[@piotr.musiol|https://taxify.atlassian.net/jira/people/6017bc27a41ea5006a86f73b] I'm not sure I'm following fully. I understand that this is related to agents failing to pick up, but I am not catching why there are cases where this happens. Apologies if we are going over this several times, trying to catch-up with the context. For the case [here|https://beehive.bolt.eu/customer_support/case/705802568] - it looks like the agent answered, I don't see a call-back?

{quote}I honestly doubt that's true. Sure, it seems that there is a technical possibility that it will happen, but declining a Push Assignment shouldn't take more than a few seconds - and we're talking only about the situation when already, for some other reason, 60 seconds were wasted.{quote}

Provided only one call gets offered. Let me ask: in the original set-up, if I'm not mistaken, we agreed that after an agent _declines_ a call, we won't offer another call for 15 seconds; if an agent times out, the agent is then set to "away". Does this logic still apply here? Meaning if an agent declines a call assignment, while already technically assigned to a call, they won't get other notifications pushed?

But regardless, the avg wait time on the market is at around 30 seconds if memory serves. Safe to assume that unfortunately even a couple of seconds of extra wait can in fact impact us on abandonment.

From my side, I can't speak to the wrap-up time - here I think it needs to be a discussion that happens in line with staffing since the availability would change - but for _live_ calls, meaning calls that have not been either answered or wrapped yet, we should not offer other simultaneous call contacts. How can we make this happen?

